 a strategic elevated position overlooking the Huatanay Valley, it served as a royal residence featuring trapezoidal alcoves and double-jambed doorways characteristic of elite Inca architecture. During the Spanish conquest, the palace was ceded to Cristobal Paullo, an Inca descendant collaborating with the Spanish crown, who built the Church of San Cristobal on the site. Declared National Cultural Heritage of Peru (R.D N.1128-2004-INC), today only foundations and terrace walls remain.'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:235547", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s002: period_name None -> ''1500+ AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:235548", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-10-02b_fields-wd3-s002: period_start None -> ''1510''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:307794", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-002: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/1cd78c0b/Sir Markham, sketch of Colcampata. Wellcome L0027837.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/1cd78c0b/Colcampata.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:338477", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4wc:p4wc-4325:chunk-0001: description None -> ''Colcampata is an archaeological site in the Cusco Region, Cusco Province and Cusco District of Peru [1]. The Quechua name is explained as qullqa, a deposit, plus pata, an elevated place or the edge of a river [1]. A decree numbered R.D 1128-2004-INC declared the site a National Cultural Heritage of Peru [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:418479", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-12_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_name ''1500+ AD'' -> ''1000 - 1500 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:418480", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-12_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_start ''1510'' -> ''1438''"}]'::jsonb),
