 within the extensive Gardberg site [1]. It is placed on a grave mound on a ridge overlooking the Valdres valley [1]. The Einang stone bears an Elder Futhark inscription, written from right to left, in Proto-Norse that has been dated to the 4th century [1]. It is the oldest runestone still standing at its original location, and it may be the earliest inscription to mention the word runo ''rune'' [1]. The generally accepted reading of the inscription was proposed by Erik Moltke in 1938 [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:163711", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-030: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/d8256f75/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/d8256f75/Einang finds.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:318340", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-014: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/d8256f75/Einang finds.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/d8256f75/Einangsteinen,_Vestre_Slidre.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('d8256f75-7b93-49b5-8910-801bd1591397'::uuid, 'total_power', '26', '27', 'card-stats-2026-11-12-rollback:d8256f75-7b93-49b5-8910-801bd1591397:total_power', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-12: total_power ''27'' restored on Einang Stone', '7aed73c7dddc86eee30b110a09073d9f', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "total_power = 26 from antiquity + fortification + cultural_influence + mystery + legacy = 3 + 3 + 10 + 9 + 1"}, {"source": "production export 3433e767f34031a8 (2026-11-12 10:36:17.26111+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 103 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07d''s own export (2026-11-07 15:22:34.233295+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28136", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0079:chunk-0001: site_type ''Rock relief/carving'' -> ''Inscription''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:87778", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-2182:chunk-0001: description \"The Einang stone is a 4th-century runestone near Fagernes in Oppland, Norway, bearing an Elder Futhark inscription in Proto-Norse that reads ''ek godagastiz runo faihido'' (''I, Godguest, painted/wrote the runes''). It is the oldest runestone still standing at its original location and may contain the earliest known use of the word ''runo'' (rune). The stone sits on a grave mound within the extensive Gardberg burial site on a ridge overlooking the Valdres valley. The verb ''faihido'' literally means ''painted,'' suggesting the original inscription was highlighted with pigment. Today the runestone is protected by glass walls and a roof.\" -> \"The Einang stone is a runestone located to the northeast of the Einangsundet strait near the village of Slidre in Vestre Slidre Municipality in Innlandet county [1]. The stone is notable for the age of its runic inscription [1]. The Einang runestone is located within the extensive Gardberg site [1]. It is placed on a grave mound on a ridge overlooking the Valdres valley [1]. The Einang stone bears an Elder Futhark inscription, written from right to left, in Proto-Norse that has been dated to the 4th century [1]. It is the oldest runestone still standing at its original location, and it may be the earliest inscription to mention the word runo ''rune'' [1]. The generally accepted reading of the inscription was proposed by Erik Moltke in 1938 [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:163711", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-030: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/d8256f75/hero.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/d8256f75/Einang finds.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:318340", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-05-002-014: thumbnail_url ''/data/images/wiki/d8256f75/Einang finds.webp'' -> ''/data/images/wiki/d8256f75/Einangsteinen,_Vestre_Slidre.webp''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('dae9bc10-f90d-45d4-9ab6-1408912b461e'::uuid, 'antiquity', '3', '2', 'card-stats-2026-11-12-rollback:dae9bc10-f90d-45d4-9ab6-1408912b461e:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-11-12: antiquity ''2'' restored on Wroxeter Stone', '184a10edb784b9556151f635254e0502', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 3 from period_start 460"}, {"source": "production export 3433e767f34031a8 (2026-11-12 10:36:17.26111+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 103 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 59556 cells (basis: wave 2026-11-07d''s own export (2026-11-07 15:22:34.233295+00))"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28084", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0067:chunk-0001: site_type ''Rock relief/carving'' -> ''Inscription''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:32779", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0005:chunk-0001: description \"The Wroxeter Stone (also known as the Cunorix Stone) is an inscribed memorial stone dated to c. 460-475 AD, ploughed up in 1967 just outside the defenses of the Roman city of Viroconium Cornoviorum. The inscription reads CVNORIX MACVSMA QVICO[L]I[N]E, translated as ''Cunorix son of Maqui-Coline'' in partly-Latinized Primitive Irish, evidencing post-Roman Irish settlement in western Britain. The name Cunorix means ''hound-king'' in Celtic and may relate to Cynric of Wessex. The stone was reused from an earlier Roman tombstone that had been incorporated into a building.\" -> \"The Wroxeter Stone is the name given to a c. 460–75 AD inscribed stone unearthed in 1967 at Wroxeter, England (earlier the Roman city of Viroconium Cornoviorum) [1]. The text reads: CVNORIX | MACVSM/A | QVICO[L]I[N]E, traditionally normalised as Cunorix macus Maqui Coline and translated as Cunorīx son of Maqqos Colinī, where Cunorīx and Maqqos Colinī are personal names [1]. Cunorīx, literally meaning ''hound-king'', is a well attested Celtic name, and may relate to the etymology of the name of Cynric of Wessex, a 6th-century king [1]. Maqui Coline was read by Kenneth H. Jackson as a rendering of Primitive Irish *Maqqī-Colinī (genitive of *Maqqos-Colinī); this name literally means ''son of holly'', but is an example of a type of name fashionable in early Irish that, despite looking like patronyms, were in fact given names [1]. It is one of a number of such names that include words for trees and is attested in later Old Irish in the form Macc-Cuilinn [1].\""}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:125583", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s010: period_name ''1 - 500 AD'' -> ''500 - 1000 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:125584", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-26b_fields-wd1-s010: period_start ''1'' -> ''501''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:163822", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "served-image-2026-09-30-030: thumbnail_url ''https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d4/Wroxeter_Roman_City_remains.JPG'' -> None"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:326204", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "import-hero-2026-10-06-005-003: thumbnail_url None -> ''/data/images/wiki/dae9bc10/Wroxeter Roman City remains.webp''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:418502", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-12_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_name ''500 - 1000 AD'' -> ''1 - 500 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:418503", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-11-12_mechanical-period-fix-s001: period_start ''501'' -> ''460''"}]'::jsonb),
